<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">467–<a href="/years/499">499</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Tuoba Hong, Yuan Hong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei reigned from 471 to 499, at first under the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>. Ruling alone from 490, he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 494, banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> dress and language at court and changed the imperial surname from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> to Yuan, promoting the sinicisation of the Xianbei elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowen-of-northern-wei.jpg" alt="Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Jason22 at zh.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Xiaowen_of_Northern_Wei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tuoba Hong was born on 13 October 467, the eldest son of Emperor Xianwen, then only thirteen, and Consort Li. He was made crown prince in 469, and his mother died the same year, probably forced to kill herself under the Northern Wei custom of putting crown princes' mothers to death. In 471 Xianwen, who favoured Taoist and Buddhist ideas, gave him the throne and became retired emperor, keeping real control. In 476 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>, resentful that Xianwen had executed her lover Li Yi, had him killed, most historians including Sima Guang believing by poison, and took the regency as Grand Empress Dowager.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under the regency the court introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-field-system">equal-field</a> system in 485, in which the state owned the land and distributed it equally to taxpaying farmers, breaking the power of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>. With the Three Elders system for compiling population registers, it gave the state a stable fiscal base and lasted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. The Grand Empress Dowager also had the emperor's grandfather Li Hui and his clan killed on a false charge of treason in 478. By about 483 Xiaowen seemed largely in charge, though she kept substantial powers until her death, which he mourned for three years.</p><p>His sinicisation aimed to centralise a multi-ethnic state. In 493 he set out on a campaign against Southern Qi and, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> officials tried to stop him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, offered to abandon it if the capital moved there. In 496 he ordered Xianbei surnames changed to Han ones, honoured eight Xianbei and five Han clans, and had offices given by clan status, over the objections of his official Li Chong. He also encouraged marriage between Xianbei and Han. His campaigns against Southern Qi in 495 and 497 to 498 were largely indecisive, though he took Wancheng and Xinye.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The reforms met resistance. In 496 Crown Prince Yuan Xun, unhappy with Han customs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>'s heat, plotted to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> and was deposed, and a second plot by Mu Tai and Lu Rui was crushed. In 497 Xiaowen made Yuan Ke crown prince and forced Yuan Xun to commit suicide. Already ill, he died on campaign on 26 April 499 after defeating the Southern Qi general Chen Xianda. His brothers kept his death secret until his body reached Luoyang, and Yuan Ke succeeded him as Emperor Xuanwu. By his will Empress Feng Run, whom he had found guilty of adultery, was forced to kill herself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tuoba founded the state of Dai and then the Northern Wei, which unified northern China in 439, patronised the Buddhist cave temples of Yungang and Longmen, and under <b>Emperor Xiaowen</b> moved its capital to Luoyang in 494 and adopted Chinese dress, language and surnames.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
